Z||000125||BAM-BAM||06-26-2001||08:21 PM||||  Ken, first off, I appriciate your input and in no way take it personaly. Also, I'm glad that you have a wonderful marriage (it's rare). However, I think you may have taken my point out of context or perhaps I wasn't really clear (I can be that way). <BR>  What I'm trying to say is. Sex isn't everything, but no Sex is and issue. An issue that is with out a doubt, the most sensitive and precarious anomaly in a relationship. I truly am a sexual animal, every woman who has ever had the pleasure of my intemacy knows that up front. I am not there for myself because that's gonna happen anyway. Armed with that knowledge and continuing forth in a relationship to the point of marriage suggests that, this is the norm and I am a willing participant. Now to begin with holding after the nuptuals is to suggest that, I needed something, I got it, now I can relax. <BR>  Perhaps it is that I have just been fooled or made impulsive decisions. But in the end I was led to believe that what I was getting was what I was getting. Only to end up on the short end. And so it goes for alot more men than not. Sex is a womans weapon, a barganing chip. I am a lover first in my relationships and all the women I have been with knew or claimed to know that. So what gives when the ring goes on? I haven't changed, I still hold to the values that I always have. I am a provider, a husband, a father, a lover and friend. Why does the lover part have to end so quickly after marriage. My wife can say anything to me or about me and I give creedence. However, The table is never balanced. Oh, it's balanced before but never after. <BR>  I am an electrician, I work with engineering products all day long. I reengineer applications all day long. The reason? Engineers know what will work in theory, the journeyman knows what will work in fact. Why? Because the journeyman has practical knowledge and the engineer has theorectical knowledge. This is not to bash anyone, just the way it is. You are a good guy Ken and I'm glad that you enjoy your successes with marriage. I'm not in the divorce court (yet) I keep trying different applications to solve my problems. The difference between our work and our lives? Our work doesn't have emotions and reasoning faculties, our wives, friends and family do. People can think and react. Engineering products can't. I got the jist of what you were saying, it was just a vague analogy.<P>Peace, B2 OUT! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  ||||reg||12
Z||000126||FieroHeather||06-26-2001||11:37 PM||||Listening to  what you gentlemen have been saying the past couple of days about sex and marriage and couples living together has really bothered me.  I am about to embark on that particular segment of my life in the near future, and I am concerned for my fiancee's and my own well being.  <P>I am an old-fashioned sort of girl, but due to financial reasons, I cannot afford to live on my own when going to school.  My fiancee has so kindly suggested that I move in with him and do my schooling from there instead of living waaaaaay back home in Barrie (two hours away from where he lives which is only 45min drive from my University). <P>Ken, is this necessiarly going to harm his and I's friendship and relationship instead of enhancing it?  I would appreciate your input/insight either here on the forum or via email if you prefer.  Anyone else have any opinions?||||reg||5
Z||000127||87 Pegasus||06-26-2001||11:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroHeather:<BR><B>Listening to  what you gentlemen have been saying the past couple of days about sex and marriage and couples living together has really bothered me.  I am about to embark on that particular segment of my life in the near future, and I am concerned for my fiancee's and my own well being.  <P>Why are you concered about your two's well being?  is the relationship in a down fall?  has something happened in it that you guys are feeling bad about?  Or is it just the moving on with the current relationship and moving on with live the bothering part?<P><BR>I am an old-fashioned sort of girl, but due to financial reasons, I cannot afford to live on my own when going to school.<P>I to am in the same boat as you. i go to school full time and can not afford to move just yet... i want to but just not yet...  i think it is ok to still live at home while at school...  just because you are still learning and not yet ready for the real world money wise...<P>My fiancee has so kindly suggested that I move in with him and do my schooling from there instead of living waaaaaay back home in Barrie (two hours away from where he lives which is only 45min drive from my University). <P>If you two are ready for that jump then i say go for it, but you have to feel that it is the right time...  do not force anything upon yourself that you do not feel is time...  Getting married is a big step for people...  my girlfriend and i would like to get married but we are not ready money wise plus we would like to find a place together to see if we can handle eachothers life style at home...  that is the biggest change you will see the other all the time, living, sleeping, fun time, rent, exspences, you trying to study while he wants something else or you studying and he has friends over being loud...  with you being in school it could get a litte hairy...  I say wait...  if not ask yourself this...  "AM I READY TO TAKE THIS JUMP...  if he really loves you then he will understand if you decide not to move in with him...  Because i aways say School comes first...  why cause more stree with bills and rent while you are in school and your parents will always welcome you home and you will always have a place to sleep and not worry about paying this on time...  I say just wait til you are out of school... you are alreay engaged if he loves you he will understand...  But this is only my opinion since i am in the same boat...  like i said just ask yourself : "AM I READY"  <P>is this necessiarly going to harm<BR>his and I's friendship and relationship <BR>(it could if you rush into something you are not ready for yet)<P>instead of enhancing it?  <BR>(if he loves you he will understand and by that your relationship will grow)<P><BR>I would appreciate your input/insight either here on the forum or via email if you prefer.  Anyone else have any opinions?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I hope i may have helped a little...<BR><P>------------------<BR>Friends don't let friends drive Corvettes!!||||reg||1
