Z||000132||gyro1s||06-27-2001||02:08 AM||||shacking up is a huge commitment. Are you ready ?  Can you keep your commitment to no sex before marriage?  Separate rooms?  Stuff happens to blur the line. I'm shacked up and have been for 4 years.  I have an ex-wife and a son from the marriage (the only good to come out of the marriage).  I won't get married again in the near future unless my shackee changes her mind and wants another kid.  parenting is what life is all about.  I am of the opinion that sex gets better with practice and when life crowds in on your relationship its hard to find the time to practice.  sex is also the only reason for the relationship you have with that person.  you can be friends with many people but in my opinion only having sex with one person ( in a commited relationship).  That being said you can only know some one when you spend a life with them. there's my two cents. <BR>jeff||||reg||1
Z||000133||Ken Wittlief||06-27-2001||03:20 PM||||FieroHeather<P>  Im not completely clear on your situation, Ive gone back and looked at some of your other post, but for young people in general let me approach this from another angle. <P>  My priorities in life are to:<P>  1. God<BR>  2. Spouse<BR>  3. my children<BR>  4. my self (career - hobbies...)<P>  God is at the top because marriage is until 'death do us part' (some people think they will be married for eternity) so in the big picture, marriage is temporary but your relationship with God is eternal.<P>  Spouse is second. When you get married it is as if the two of you become one person. People invite you over as a couple from then on - you do almost everything together - when one person is sad or ill or going through a rough time, the other is affected almost as if it were happening to them. So you cant separate anything about your spouse from yourself, anymore than you can go away for the day and leave your legs home - the two of you become one person in a very real sense.<P>  Children are 3rd - they are an excellent example of how two people can become one person - execept in this case two people become a third person who is separate from you. A living creation of your love for each other.<P>  To start towards answering your question, you have to decide why you are moving in together - what is the most important aspect of this - to save money, to enable you to attend college, to get away from your parents, as a trial marriage...?<P>  Whether your relationship is sexual yet or not: look at the situation from someone living across the street from his apartment. They will see a young guy and a young lady living together in the same place - It would be perfectly logical to assume the two of you are married - you will be depending on each other, sharing meals, cars, doing stuff on weekends - and also going through rough times together - being short on money, illnesses, family emergencies... So from the outside it sure looks like a marriage.<P>  Or look at it this way. If Spouse is #2 on your list, and someday you end up being married to someone - if its not this guy, then how will you explain this part of your life to him:<P>  A. you are sort of my second husband, but not really.<P>  B. we lived together but we never had sex<P>  C. I was going through a desperate time and had no other choice<P>  D. ?<P> many people think "we will live together to see how it goes first, and then if it doesnt work out we'll split up". But they dont look one day ahead after that. Where are you then? how is that emotionally or financially different from being married and then splitting up? if you split up you will be still be emotionally devistated and you will have nowhere to go.<P>  Everyone has heard the song Edilwise (sic) the haunting german song about the flower - the real story behind that song is those flowers only grow naturally up in the alps - way up near the tops of the mountains. It was traditional for a young man to trek up to the top of a mountain to find a flower, and to bring one back for his true love. It was an extravagent gesture - a great risk - and sometimes the young man would fall to his death and never return.<P>   Why so much danger and risk over a stupid flower?! its not the flower - its a symbol of his dedication - the depth of his love - a way to show 'I will do anything for you - you are more important to me than my own life' and I will risk everything to make you happy - even if its only for a moment.<P>   I heard it expressed once that there are only two things that are eternal: God, and a human soul. All the other things that seem so important to us now pale when we realize what we are doing now is temporary - but our souls are eternal - you dont know what your career will be ten years from now, or where you will live, what your health will be, how many children you will have, who your friends will be.<P>  In the long run the only thing that will really matter is the souls in your life - your spouse, your children, your friends and coworkers - they are eternal - even though you are only married until 'death do us part' the relationship you have with your spouse is something you will remember for eternity. It will be a part of you forever.<P> We will dedicate four years of our lives to get a degree for our career - how come we dont spend that kind of energy on our spouses - our future spouses, our children? Making sure our relationship with them is the best it can possible be?<P>[BTW im not hinting that your soul is in danger if you live together - I am saying the quality of your interaction with other souls is forever, you career and your possesions are temporary]<P>   So I guess what you need to ask yourself is: are you ready to marry this guy? If not, do you want to do something that will compromise the marriage you will have eventually, whether its with him or someone else?<P>  Marriage is not something you wade into like a long sloping beach, where you put your toe in, then walk out to your ankles, then out to your knees...<P>   Its more like a 50 foot cliff with deep water below - you take a long look - take your best guess how deep the water is, and you plunge headfirst - or you stay on the cliff.<P>  If you were my daughter, and you were thinking of moving in with some guy (whether the situation is sexual or not) my personal advice to you would be, either take the plunge and get married, or go the hard way, take the long walk along the cliff alone and go around the lake (get a part time job - goto school partime - get your own place - struggle it out by yourself).<P>  I dont know why people think they have to wait for their personal situation to be at its best before being married... not enough money yet, must finish school first... <P>going through the difficult parts of life together is as much a part of being married as going through the easy and fun parts (actually - more so). If you think "I have to make $X thousand dollars a year and own my own house before I should get married", then what about all the people that will never make that much money? What if something happens to you and you cant work anymore later? is the marriage dissolved then? <P>Maybe some people need to get their lives together before getting married (find themself), but when you reach the point where you have taken stock of where you are, and you decide to go from here and make the best out of what you have - this is who I am and this is where I want to go - then that is pretty much 'where' you will be for the rest of your life - moving forward, making the best of things.<P>  if you take the second approach (stay on the cliff) you can always take the plunge later - whenever the two of you feel you are ready.<P>   And if your relationship is already sexual - if the genie is already out of the bottle - then that doesnt necessarilly mean your relationship is doomed - as long as you understand it has already been stressed - it has already been compromised - if you two are willing to work at it - shore it up - accept the fact that you have taken the shortcut and now you will have extra work and effort to do - it can still work <P> a lot of the magic will be missing (did you ever notice there is something odd about a wedding when the couple has been living together for a while already?)<P>   So yes you can move in together and it can be made to work out in the long run - but it will be more difficult for both of you because you are taking the easy path now, and putting off 'paying your dues' until later. You can go this route if you want to - just make sure you realize and accept that this is what you are doing.<P>  Thats one of the great things about life - we dont die the instant we make our first mistake - we can start off on the wrong foot and recover - we can mess stuff up really bad and then pull it out of the mud and make it better - but you do have to realize its in the mud before you can pull it out and wash it off - so if the genie is all ready out of the bottle then all is NOT lost.<P>  [let me say one thing on the side here. Some people will read this post and say: he only feels this way cause he believes in God...<P>  God doenst put down rules like : no sex before marriage - because he is a spoilsport and wants to ruin all our fun - its because he wants us to enjoy our lives to the fullest - He knows things about us that we dont understand - things we wont realize until later - for what seems best to us now, He knows the right way. <P>If you put off sex until after marriage you will get the MOST out of it - just like if you put off driving your car until AFTER you put oil in the motor: you will get the most enjoyment out of your car. Sure it will runs like crazy without oil - for a few minutes - for the first minute or so you wont even know the difference - but in the long run you're gonna spend many hours in the garage rebuilding the motor, and then I hope you put oil in it before you drive it again.]<P>If you feel like you are ready then go the whole nine yards, make the commitment, take the plunge (yes its scary - its scary for everyone - if you wait till you are 100% sure you will wait your whole life - thats why its a plunge - sink or swim - theres no climbing back up the cliff if the water is too cold).||||reg||1
Z||000134||Voytek||06-27-2001||04:10 PM||||Ken's got some excellent points.  I couldn't agree more, on virtually all of them.  Here's an example:  someone I work with has lived together for about 1.5 years before they got married.  The day of the wedding, other than an unusual amount of guests, a nice dress/suit and a ring, nothing was different.  They left the house together to go to church (and even had a small argument shortly before that), and after the party just came back to the same house - life continued as if nothing had been changed.  She told me she cried for a couple of days - not from happiness.  The point is, shacking up only takes away from the magic of marriage.  In fact, perhaps both of you will decide that living together is just as good and never get married, etc, etc.  I guess I don't know you FieroHeather, but, even though I'm still pretty young, I've seen this sort of thing a few times.  Life together before marriage makes marriage dull.  The only thing I may not agree with Ken on is the money thing.  I mean, trying to get a whole career going before any marriage commitments may take a loooong time, but on the other hand, there's nothing wrong with planning go have a good job and education prior to marriage.  Too many marriages have fallen apart because of tough financial times.  I'm not saying you should be rich, but having a good steady income ensures a peace of mind so you can concentrate on your relationship rather than where to make that extra buck.  My point is, patience is good, any way you look at it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000135||Ken Wittlief||06-27-2001||04:30 PM||||Id agree with that. For many people the college years are a time when you are still figuring out who you are. I would not advise 18 year old highschool sweethearts to get married before they go to college, and then goto college together. <P>  but if you are 22 - 25 and you have been out on your own for a while, and you have a good idea of what you want to do with your life - then starting off a marriage on a shoestring is not a bad thing - definately better than living together.<P>  You can always make plans to goto Hawaii for a second honeymoon on your 5th anniversary (c:||||reg||1
